# codex-scientiae — git attributes
#
# Every pattern here answers exactly one of three questions, asked in this order:
#
#   1. Are its exact bytes the contract?   -> -text          freeze; never normalize
#   2. Is it binary?                       -> binary         freeze, and no textual diff
#   3. Is it text we edit?                 -> text eol=lf    normalize to LF in the repo
#
# Question 1 is the one generic templates miss and the one this repository actually needs. A file
# can be perfectly readable text and still belong in category 1 -- a byte-exact test fixture,
# acquired source whose SHA-256 is recorded as provenance, or any format the engine itself owns.
#
# That last case is the important one. Where code declares a file's bytes, git must not also have
# an opinion about them: two authorities over one artifact is not belt-and-braces, it is a silent
# contradiction waiting for the first artifact whose declared policy differs from git's default.
# Ambient normalization is for files nothing else governs.
#
# This file governs bytes in the REPOSITORY, for TRACKED files, at checkout and commit. It has no
# authority over generated artifacts that are untracked, and it is never read at runtime.
#
# Ordering matters: later patterns win. Broad defaults first, specific overrides last.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Normalize anything git detects as text, and check it out as LF. A safety net so a new source type
# does not silently accumulate CRLF before anyone thinks to list it below.
#
# eol=lf is on the catch-all deliberately: without it an unlisted filetype inherits the worktree
# form from whatever core.autocrlf the cloning machine happens to have, and this repository's
# conventions should not depend on a setting that does not travel with a clone. With it, every rule
# here holds against a hostile ambient config in both directions.
#
# A file that genuinely needs CRLF must say so rather than rely on the default -- .bat and .cmd are
# the real cases (cmd.exe can mis-parse an LF-only batch file around labels). None exist here yet;
# add `*.bat text eol=crlf` alongside if one does.
* text=auto eol=lf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Text we edit — normalized to LF
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Documents and data. Formats the engine owns are NOT here -- see category 1.
*.md        text eol=lf diff=markdown
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.csv       text eol=lf
*.xml       text eol=lf
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# PowerShell
*.ps1       text eol=lf
*.psm1      text eol=lf
*.psd1      text eol=lf

# Python
*.py        text eol=lf diff=python

# C# and MSBuild
*.cs        text eol=lf diff=csharp
*.csproj    text eol=lf
*.props     text eol=lf
*.targets   text eol=lf
*.sln       text eol=lf

# JavaScript / TypeScript
*.js        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.tsx       text eol=lf

# Repository metadata and editor config
.gitignore          text eol=lf
.gitattributes      text eol=lf
.copilotignore      text eol=lf
*.code-workspace    text eol=lf
LICENSE             text eol=lf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Binary — never touched, no textual diff
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.gz        binary
*.zip       binary
*.tar       binary
*.dll       binary
*.exe       binary
*.pyc       binary

# JSOI byte-offset index: int64 offsets, no text anywhere in it. Marked explicitly because a
# misdetect that ran EOL conversion over the offset table would corrupt every seek in the store.
*.jidx      binary


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Bytes are the contract — text-shaped, frozen anyway
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Formats the engine owns. jsonl_engine declares encoding, escaping, and record terminator per
# artifact and records all three in the .sig; the reader then enforces what was declared. Git
# normalizing these would not be redundant with that, it would contradict it: a kind that declares
# EOL = Eol.CRLF would have its store rewritten to LF on checkout, and the reader would refuse the
# store it just wrote. One authority over these bytes, and it is the code.
*.jsonl                             -text
*.json                              -text
*.sig                               -text

# Byte-exact reference data. A golden is compared against fresh output, so whatever it was when it
# was captured is the thing under test -- the jsonl-engine CRLF golden would be rewritten to LF on
# commit and then pass vacuously. Named, not located: the suffix travels with the file.
*.golden                            -text

# Acquired LaTeX source. source_forms in the article schema records a SHA-256 over the archive and
# over the extracted tree, so normalizing a .tex on commit would invalidate provenance for a file
# this project did not author. Frozen as received.
#
# By extension rather than by path: acquired trees move, and a path rule goes stale silently while
# an extension rule keeps holding wherever the source lands.
*.tex                               -text
*.sty                               -text
*.cls                               -text
*.bib                               -text
*.bbl                               -text
*.bst                               -text


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Whitespace strictness
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.ts    whitespace=trailing-space,blank-at-eol,blank-at-eof
*.tsx   whitespace=trailing-space,blank-at-eol,blank-at-eof
*.js    whitespace=trailing-space,blank-at-eol,blank-at-eof
*.py    whitespace=trailing-space,blank-at-eol,blank-at-eof
*.cs    whitespace=trailing-space,blank-at-eol,blank-at-eof
*.ps1   whitespace=trailing-space,blank-at-eol,blank-at-eof

# Generated or minified output is exempt from whitespace checks.
*.min.js    -whitespace
*.g.cs      -whitespace
